James sought alternatives to the terms "market losses" and "non-market losses" for his report on health impacts, wishing to use language accessible to a general audience. I suggested more relatable terms: for "market losses," alternatives included "Economic Costs" and "Financial Impacts," emphasizing financial implications. For "non-market losses," I offered "Wellbeing Costs," "Life Quality Impacts," "Human Costs," and "Value of Life Impacts," focusing on personal experiences and broader human implications. These suggestions aimed to clarify the distinction between direct financial consequences and intangible impacts on wellbeing, making the concepts more approachable for non-experts.
